      "writtenOn": "2023-09-14T16:59:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I did a little digging, and it seems as if we don\u0027t write a response to the requester. In that case, how does the requester know what went wrong? Do we just close the connection on them and they just give up w/o knowing what happened?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 40,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 40,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "cd81ebc2a225d94e71570a1223b3e82fd5bea274",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fff07895_f669526e",
        "filename": "rialto/src/requests/api.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 40,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-15T13:33:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Currently, we are missing a mechanism to propagate errors from the RKP VM to the host. In the event of a failure, the host receives a generic failure message from the VM and subsequently shuts it down. However, due to the generic nature of the failure, the host cannot determine whether it is an invalid MAC error or not. This is why I want to introduce RemoteProvisioningError.\n\nAlternatively, instead of creating a new `RemoteProvisioningError`, it is also feasible to forward all `RequestProcessingError` to the host. This approach may be cleaner. What about like this \u003d\u003e https://r.android.com/c/2753747",
      "parentUuid": "408bc29d_8d8176cc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 40,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 40,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "cd81ebc2a225d94e71570a1223b3e82fd5bea274",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2c5270e6_e0d39db9",
        "filename": "rialto/src/requests/api.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
